\[
\begin{aligned}
a&=(0,0,0),& b&=(-1,0,0),& c&=(1,0,0),\\
e&=(0,1,0),& d&=(1,1,0),\\
r_0&=(0,-1,0),&r_+&=(0,0,1),&r_-&=(0,0,-1).
\end{aligned}
\]

The selected birth edge is \((a,b)\). The six neighbors of \(a\) are exactly \(b,c,e,r_0,r_+,r_-\); \(d\) is a common A neighbor of \(c,e\). Periods at least six keep these sites and the plaquette distinct.

In both matter words put every A site in \(+1\), put \(r_0\) in \(+1\), put \(r_+,r_-\) in \(-1\), and leave all unlisted B sites vacant. Define the remaining entries by

| Matter word | \(c\) | \(e\) | \(b\) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| \(q_c\) | \(+1\) | \(0\) | \(0\) |
| \(q_e\) | \(0\) | \(+1\) | \(0\) |

Both words have \(|A|+4\) records and total signed charge \(|A|\). The four occupied B records have zero net charge. This is essential on a torus: adding an uncompensated B charge to all-A-plus data would violate the total Gauss constraint.

Define

\[
\begin{split}
\beta&=e_{a r_+}+e_{a r_-}-e_{a r_0},\\
\ell&=e_{ac}-e_{dc}+e_{de}-e_{ae},\\
\eta_e&=\beta-e_{ae},\\
\eta_c&=\beta+e_{de}-e_{ae}-e_{dc}.
\end{split}                                                       \tag{3}
\]

The vector \(\ell\) is the circulation around the elementary square \(a,c,d,e,a\). It has zero divergence and zero winding. Directly,

\[
\begin{split}
\operatorname{div}\eta_c&=\delta_c+\delta_{r_0}-\delta_{r_+}-\delta_{r_-},\\
\operatorname{div}\eta_e&=\delta_e+\delta_{r_0}-\delta_{r_+}-\delta_{r_-}.
\end{split}                                                       \tag{4}
\]

Thus, for every divergence-free reference field \(\phi\), both \(|q_v\rangle U_{\eta_v}\phi\) satisfy the physical Gauss law. On that reference field space define the isometry

\[
V_-\phi=\frac{|q_c\rangle U_{\eta_c}\phi
                    -|q_e\rangle U_{\eta_e}\phi}{\sqrt2}.           \tag{5}
\]

The matter words are orthogonal, so \(V_-^*V_-=I\) regardless of the overlap of the two electric vectors. It also prepares arbitrary mixed reference fields by \(\rho\mapsto V_-\rho V_-^*\). The finite electric shifts preserve normalizability. One may choose a zero-winding reference field as in the weak-field parent; no extra homogeneous winding constraint is imposed on the resulting charged matter sectors.

